---

### 🔷 **React-na enna?**

**React** is a **JavaScript library** for building **user interfaces** (mostly frontend). Facebook develop pannadhu, and it's component-based. That means, UI-a small reusable parts-a divide panni build pannuvinga.

---

## 🧩 **Frontend – React Setup & UI Creation**

### ✅ Step 1: Install Node.js
- Go to [https://nodejs.org](https://nodejs.org) and install Node.js (comes with `npm` – Node package manager)

### ✅ Step 2: Create React App
Open terminal and run:
```bash
npx create-react-app my-app
cd my-app
npm start
```
This will start a development server at `http://localhost:3000`

### ✅ Step 3: Build UI Components
React app structure:
```
/src
  └── App.js        <-- main component
  └── components/   <-- reusable UI parts (optional folder)
```

#### Example: Simple Login Form Component
```jsx
// src/components/LoginForm.js
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const LoginForm = () => {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [password, setPassword] = useState('');

  const handleSubmit = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    console.log({ email, password });
    // API call to backend can go here
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input type="email" value={email} onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} placeholder="Email" />
      <input type="password" value={password} onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)} placeholder="Password" />
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
  );
};

export default LoginForm;
```

---

## 🔧 **Backend – Node.js with Express**

### ✅ Step 1: Create a backend folder

```bash
mkdir backend
cd backend
npm init -y
npm install express cors body-parser
```

### ✅ Step 2: Basic Express Server

```js
// backend/index.js
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');

const app = express();
app.use(cors());
app.use(bodyParser.json());

app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const { email, password } = req.body;
  // Validate user (dummy)
  if (email === 'test@example.com' && password === '123456') {
    res.json({ status: 'success' });
  } else {
    res.json({ status: 'failure' });
  }
});

app.listen(5000, () => console.log('Server running on port 5000'));
```

---

## 🔄 **Frontend <--> Backend Connection**

In your React frontend (`LoginForm.js`):

```js
fetch('http://localhost:5000/login', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email, password }),
})
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```

---

## 🗃️ **Database Connection (MongoDB Example)**

### ✅ Step 1: Install Mongoose

In your backend project:
```bash
npm install mongoose
```

### ✅ Step 2: Connect to MongoDB

```js
// backend/index.js (add this at top)
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/myapp', {
  useNewUrlParser: true,
  useUnifiedTopology: true,
});
```

### ✅ Step 3: Create User Schema

```js
const UserSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  email: String,
  password: String,
});
const User = mongoose.model('User', UserSchema);

// Modify login route to use DB
app.post('/login', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await User.findOne({ email: req.body.email, password: req.body.password });
  if (user) {
    res.json({ status: 'success' });
  } else {
    res.json({ status: 'failure' });
  }
});
```

---

## 🔗 **Quick Summary**

| Part        | Tool/Library           | Description |
|-------------|------------------------|-------------|
| Frontend    | React                  | UI rendering |
| Backend     | Node.js + Express      | API and logic |
| Database    | MongoDB + Mongoose     | Data storage |
| Communication | `fetch()` or `axios` | Frontend talks to backend |

---

