Let’s solve it 🔧

You're **almost there**, but there’s a **critical mistake in the order** of your code:

---

## ❌ Bug:
You’re calling `app.use(...)` **before** initializing `app` — see this:

```js
const contactRoutes = require("./routes/contactRoutes"); // ✅ file name
app.use("/api/contacts", contactRoutes); // ❌ app is not defined yet

const app = express(); // ← initialized too late
```

---

## ✅ Fix:

Move the `app.use(...)` **after** `const app = express();`

---

### ✅ Corrected Version:

```js
const express = require("express");
const mongoose = require("mongoose");
const cors = require("cors");
const contactRoutes = require("./routes/contactRoutes"); // ✅ file name

const app = express(); // ✅ Initialize app BEFORE using it

// Middleware
app.use(cors());
app.use(express.json());

// Routes
app.use("/api/contacts", contactRoutes); // ✅ After app is defined

// MongoDB connection
mongoose.connect("mongodb+srv://munendhirasn:munendhirasn@cluster0.0yc23ah.mongodb.net/contact-manager?retryWrites=true&w=majority")
  .then(() => console.log("✅ MongoDB connected"))
  .catch((err) => console.error("❌ MongoDB connection error:", err));

// Start server
app.listen(5000, () => {
  console.log("🚀 Backend running on http://localhost:5000");
});
```

---

Now re-run the backend using:

```bash
node index.js
```

Then go to your frontend and refresh — it should work correctly now.

